WHAT "100%" CAN MEAN
- Every one of the 6,110 catalog courses is checked against every enumerable public source, and ends with a verdict, a reason code and the list of sources checked.
- Every file in every enumerable public store is captured. These stores have an index or a finite ID space:
  - getsyllabus 1-4,400 and getfile 1-1,300;
  - app.sib, app.mcb and the Math topics index;
  - the Drupal /document/ ranges on math, chemistry, economics, stat and psychology;
  - the Canvas course-id space;
  - Wayback CDX for every known host.

WHAT IT CANNOT MEAN
1. A syllabus for every course. After everything, about 13-15% of catalog courses (17-19% of Fall 2026 courses) will have a current public syllabus, and 20-23% any full syllabus. Most syllabi live in Canvas, where about 99% of course shells require login. Others sit in Box folders that need a UIUC login (Political Science, English, Rhetoric, the Gen Ed examples), in Moodle content, in the ACES office's files, or in CIM course proposals. CIM proposals are the one complete official store, because every new course must attach a syllabus, but catalog.illinois.edu's robots.txt disallows /cim/, /courseleaf/ and /courseadmin/.
2. Finding every syllabus that exists somewhere public. Personal sites, Google Drive, Dropbox and GitHub pages that no crawlable page links to can only be found by web search. Search is capped at 200 queries per session; that budget was used up during this research. Search engines also skip pages marked noindex, and search missed 3 of 13 syllabi that do exist in the Grainger stores. So "exhaustive" means exhaustive over enumerable sources, with best-effort search on top.
3. Current syllabi everywhere. Much of what exists is old:
   - Wayback copies are mostly 2009-2013 and 2017-2025;
   - Math term syllabi stop in 2022;
   - History guides stop in 2017;
   - EUI stops in 2011;
   - the UP archive stops in Fall 2024.
   Multi-section courses have a different syllabus per instructor, so the one shown may not match a student's section. Show the term and section on every fact.
4. Complete archives. The Wayback Machine holds only what it happened to capture. The domain-wide walk that would list everything takes 4-5 days. The CDX filter only catches URLs containing 'syllab'.
5. Perfect extraction. Deterministic rules will leave roughly 20-40% of fields uncertain: tables in PDFs, points-based grading, and prose policies. There is no OCR on this machine for image-only PDFs. JavaScript-only public sites (Notion, cs124.org, learncs.online) need a headless renderer that is not installed.
6. Anything behind a login, paywall, CAPTCHA or bot wall. This covers:
   - Canvas private shells;
   - Box logins;
   - SAML-gated Math, NetMath and Moodle content;
   - Course Hero and Studocu;
   - Open Syllabus (behind a Cloudflare challenge);
   - the Illini Union Bookstore (a bot wall);
   - online.uillinois.edu (Cloudflare);
   - IDEALS, which returns 403 to the truthful user agent.
7. ECON Box contents. They come only through the user's own browser download or the department, because Box's robots.txt disallows the download path.

DECISIONS FOR THE USER
- Canvas and Box pages carry meta noindex,nofollow. This is not a robots.txt rule, but the design republishes only facts plus a link and does not follow links on those pages. The user should confirm that is acceptable.
- Whether to supply the ECON Box files.
- Whether to spend about 24 hours on the Canvas sweep, which adds only an estimated 5-40 new courses.

ISSUES FOUND ALONG THE WAY
1. scripts/illinois/excellent-teachers.mjs already downloads from https://uofi.box.com/index.php?rm=box_download_shared_file..., a path uofi.box.com's robots.txt disallows (I re-checked on 2026-09-27: "User-agent: *", "Disallow: /", with Allow only for /s/, /shared/, /v/ and a few asset paths). This breaks the house "respects robots.txt" rule; the user should decide whether to fix it.
2. The humanities researcher fetched 13 ECON files through Box's anonymous preview token on public.boxcloud.com. That route sidesteps the disallowed download endpoint and should not be used or ingested.
3. The archives researcher made one request to the disallowed courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/ path before checking robots.txt.
4. The sciences researcher ran chemistry.illinois.edu at about 2 requests per second for about 25 minutes.
5. No researcher swept 770 catalog courses in 66 subjects.

My own traffic: I made 5 requests today, the Box robots.txt and 4 HEAD requests to getsyllabus ids 4036-4040. I edited nothing in the planner.
